{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310626,"nonce":"RMu+xRM8eP6Exv+2WxnHAVWzEsc=","title":"Investigation log of the 6 failing/crashing tests in the haxe e2e suite as of 2026-05-19. Pre-existed Phase 3a foundation work. Each ...","message":"Snapshot 2026-05-19 — `./run_haxe_tests.sh` reports 122 pass / 3 fail / 3 crash. These are pre-existing; Phase 3a foundation (commit 759e161) was confirmed at full parity. Below is the per-test root-cause analysis.\n\n\n`var a = [for (i in 0...3) i]` produces an empty array (length 0). Downstream `a[0]` segfaults because the data ptr is null.\n\n**Root cause**: commit b1ac79e (2026-02-27 \"Fix comprehension lowering and generic extern monomorphization dispatch\") changed the desugaring from `_tmp.push(expr)` to `_tmp[_idx] = expr; _idx += 1`. The new path is broken because `_tmp` becomes a loop-carried SSA value via phi nodes in `lower_for_in_range` ([compiler/src/ir/hir_to_mir.rs:27849+](compiler/src/ir/hir_to_mir.rs#L27849)) — but the loop body never *re-assigns* the array variable (mutation is hidden inside `haxe_array_set_i64`). The phi's back-edge equals the phi itself, the exit phi reads stale state, downstream length reads 0.\n\n**Workaround for users**: write the loop explicitly with `var t:Array\u003cInt\u003e = []; for (i in 0...n) t[i] = expr;` — that works (verified `/tmp/test_arrset_loop.hx`).\n\n**Attempted fix (reverted)**: I tried reverting the desugaring to push-based, but my synthesized `arr.push(x)` HirExprKind::Call doesn't dispatch correctly. The user-written `arr.push(x)` works, but the synthetic call with looked-up SymbolId from `lookup_array_push_method` resolves to a push symbol that the MIR Call lowering doesn't bind to `haxe_array_push_*`. The difference is likely in the callee's `expr.ty` or the receiver type metadata that `get_stdlib_method_info` expects. Needs deeper investigation than a quick revert allows.\n\n**Fix path forward**: Either (a) fix the phi loop-carried value bug in `lower_for_in_range` so unmodified loop-carried pointers don't degrade to degenerate phis, or (b) re-implement the comprehension desugaring to bypass HIR by emitting MIR runtime calls directly. (a) is cleaner — the phi degenerate case probably affects other loops too.\n\n\n`class MyException extends Exception { public function new(msg) { super(msg); } }` — the `super(msg)` call fails to resolve the parent constructor. Likely the recent RTTI work (10354a5, 82e9b80) shifted TypeId numbering and the super-call lookup uses a stale TypeId. Affects any user-defined Exception subclass.\n\n\nCrashes immediately, no trace output. Likely BLADE cache cross-module function reference issue (similar pattern to the 2026-03-09 fix). The `external_function_names` resolution may be missing for some haxe.Json paths.\n\n\nThe Cranelift JIT backend can't resolve `set` symbol when linking. The `set` method on ObjectMap is referenced but the runtime mapping or symbol export is missing. Likely a stdlib mapping gap (`ObjectMap.set` not in `runtime_mapping.rs` for the right param count).\n\n\nStringTools methods (length, trim, ltrim, rtrim, isSpace, hex, lpad, rpad) are all failing class registration. Similar pattern to the @:forward abstract issue. The StringTools class probably isn't being registered when imported, possibly because of a recent import resolution change.\n\n\nRoot cause was simpler than predicted: NOT about Thread.spawn or Send — `var f = static_fn; f()` SIGSEGV'd unconditionally. Variable lowering for SymbolKind::Function emitted `build_function_ptr` (raw fn_addr) but CallIndirect expects a `{fn_ptr, env_ptr}` closure object. Switched to `build_function_ref` which wraps in the closure-object layout. See [[bugs-function-ref-indirect-call]].\n\n\n- Failures 3, 5 smell like **stdlib symbol/class registration** issues. Worth checking if the BLADE cache name-based external function fixup (commit 2026-03-09) covers all stdlib reload paths.\n- Failures 1, 6 smell like **MIR lowering** issues for newer features (loop-carried SSA, Function-value send).\n- Failure 2 is **TypeId stability** across compilation context — same family as the 2026-03-09 `class_alloc_sizes_by_name` fix.\n- Failure 4 is **stdlib_mapping coverage** — most tractable; add the missing mapping entry.\n\n\nAfter Phase 3a foundation (commit 759e161), the suite remains at 122/128. No new regressions; no fixes either. The 6 failures need targeted per-cluster work.\n\n\n- Map\u003cK,V\u003e.get/set (commit e26eab8) → +1\n- test_thread_spawn_function_value (commit 4882aee) → +1\n- Current baseline: 124/128 passing. Remaining 4: test_array_comprehension_scope (SIGSEGV), test_exception (exit 1), test_json (SIGSEGV), test_stringbuf_stringtools (exit 1). test_objectmap is no longer in the failure set (Map\u003cK,V\u003e fix subsumed it).\n- **Cache caveat**: `run_haxe_tests.sh` only clears `.rayzor/blade/cache/*.blade`, NOT `.rayzor/cache/*.mir.cache`. After a fix to MIR lowering, also `rm -rf .rayzor/cache/` to invalidate stale MIR or the regression will appear to still fail.\n\n\n- **test_exception** — super-call lookup added a SymbolId-derived fallback in `register_class_metadata` so canonicalised parent TypeIds (from `tast_to_hir.rs:extends_canonical = TypeId::from_raw(parent_sym.as_raw())`) can resolve to the parent class symbol even when `type_table.get(extends_type_id)` returns None.\n- **test_array_comprehension_scope** — RD parser never built `Pattern::ArrayComprehension`; `[for (i in ...) expr]` parsed as `Array([For{...}])`. Added a `for`-prefix branch in `parse_array_or_map` that calls a new `parse_comprehension_for` helper.\n- **test_json** — `haxe.Json.parse` runtime mapping declared its String parameter as `PtrU8`, which triggered the auto-box-to-Dynamic path. Changed to `PtrString` so the raw `*const HaxeString` flows through unchanged.\n\n\n- **test_stringbuf_stringtools** — commit `a332319`. Final hypothesis was wrong; actual root cause was simpler: BLADE saved orphan property entries with `class_name=\"\"` (from `find_class_name_for_scope` returning None for some props), and the name-based fallback in `lower_field_access` returned them first due to BTreeMap iteration order — shadowing the real `Method(\"get_length\")` entry with a `Default` getter. Save side now skips orphan entries; lookup side prefers Method over Default. See [[bugs-blade-property-orphan]].\n- **Suite is now 128/128 in batch AND isolation.** No remaining haxe regressions.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_haxe_regression_122_of_128` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}